d workers already in  Germany should be exhausted first but  the ruling Coalition disagrees will pull  out all stops to raise the potential at  home so that a shortage of skilled  workers does not become a break on  growth and when we've done with all of  that it won't be enough we need  supplementary qualified immigration from  abroad  the aim is to allow 400 000 people to  immigrate every year quickly digitally  and unbureaucratically a draft bill will  be presented to the bundestag at the  beginning of next year  and from war now let's bring in DW  political correspondent Julia Sao Delhi  so we're hearing there the German  government appearing to have some  decisive measures in mind why now  well the three parties that are in the  uh coalition government at the moment  had already agreed on wanting to reform  Germany's migration laws already last  year when they signed a coalition  agreement but it's becoming ever clear  that Germany really is an urgent need of  more foreign workers if we look at the  numbers one in two companies in Germany  are suffering from a lack of skilled  workers in all kinds of sectors from  services to I.T manufacturing to health  care and the situation is expected to  get even worse if and no measures are  taken because of Germany's aging  population more people going into  retirement and the low birth rate that's  why we've seen the proposal of this  points-based system to allow foreign  workers outside of the EU to come to  Germany if they respect certain criteria  which depends on qualifications and  degrees work experience experience a  language skills skills and age and that  is meant to make the process easier for  skilled workers from abroad to come to  Germany but also to expand the pool of  qualified workers who can come and work  in Germany  and there are other pro-immigration  measures in the pipeline what's on the  table  yes so on the one hand the the Coalition  has put forward a work in progress is a  draft to make naturalization easier for  people who are already living in Germany  the process would be shortened from  eight to five years people would then be  able to apply for citizenship after five  years of living in Germany and it would  also allow a dual citizenship with  countries outside the EU and Switzerland  this is something that is not allowed  currently and that would for example in  particular impact the the big community  in in Germany with Turkish Roots who  have until now had to decide between a  Turkish and a German passport but their  planned reforms also look at the Asylum  process which the current government  wants to accelerate make faster less  bureaucratic and and more efficient and  it also looks at this  category of people that are under  so-called tolerated status that means  these are people whose Asylum claim has  been rejected but these people have not  been deported because of humanitarian or  technical reasons and now these people  if they've been in Germany for at least  five years would now receive a residence  permit on a one-year probation and that  is aimed to acknowledge the fact that  these people have built a life in  Germany in the last five years and that  they have a chance to to continue to  live in Germany and to stay here Julia  saudeli in Berlin thank you
 

